  "raw_table_text": "Table 1. Participants’ characteristics according to depressive symptoms\nAll\nwithout depressivesymptoms\nwith depressive symptoms\np value\n(N = 20,142)\n(N = 17,753)\n(N = 2,389)\nAge, years\nMean age (SD), y\n74.5 (6.2)\n74.4 (6.1)\n75.4 (6.6)\n<0.001\n60–69\n4,644\n23.1%\n4,150\n23.4%\n494\n20.7%\n<0.001\n70–79\n11,068\n54.9%\n9,842\n55.4%\n1,226\n51.3%\n80–89\n4,235\n21.0%\n3,607\n20.3%\n628\n26.3%\n≥90\n195\n1.0%\n154\n0.9%\n41\n1.7%\nSex\nFemale\n10,893\n54.1%\n9,643\n54.3%\n1,250\n52.3%\n0.066\nMale\n9,249\n45.9%\n8,110\n45.7%\n1,139\n47.7%\nMean number of prescribed medications (SD)\n3.4 (3.0)\n3.2 (2.9)\n4.2 (3.4)\n<0.001\nMissing\n36\n0.2%\n32\n0.2%\n4\n0.2%\nDisease history\nHypertension\n9,606\n47.7%\n8,388\n47.2%\n1,218\n51.0%\n<0.001\nDiabetes\n2,799\n13.9%\n2,415\n13.6%\n384\n16.1%\n0.001\nStroke\n1,111\n5.5%\n908\n5.1%\n203\n8.5%\n<0.001\nRespiratory disease\n2,572\n12.8%\n2,205\n12.4%\n367\n15.4%\n<0.001\nMean MMSE score (SD), points\n27.1 (2.3)\n27.2 (2.3)\n26.7 (2.5)\n<0.001\nEquivalized income, JPY\n>2 million\n8,583\n42.6%\n7,341\n41.4%\n1,242\n52.0%\n<0.001\n2–4 million\n7,951\n39.5%\n7,164\n40.4%\n787\n32.9%\n≥4 million\n2,077\n10.3%\n1,952\n11.0%\n125\n5.2%\nMissing\n1,531\n7.6%\n1,296\n7.3%\n235\n9.8%\nLiving alone\nNo\n16,302\n80.9%\n14,538\n81.9%\n1,764\n73.8%\n<0.001\nYes\n3,599\n17.9%\n3,023\n17.0%\n576\n24.1%\nMissing\n241\n1.2%\n192\n1.1%\n49\n2.1%\nRegular exercise\n≥1 d/wk\n15,888\n78.9%\n14,263\n80.3%\n1,625\n68.0%\n<0.001\n<1 d/wk\n4,245\n21.1%\n3,482\n19.6%\n763\n31.9%\nMissing\n9\n0.0%\n8\n0.0%\n1\n0.0%\nSleep satisfaction\nSatisfied\n15,989\n79.4%\n14,511\n81.7%\n1,478\n61.9%\n<0.001\nDissatisfied\n4,150\n20.6%\n3,239\n18.2%\n911\n38.1%\nMissing\n3\n0.0%\n3\n0.0%\n0\n0.0%\nAlcohol consumption\nNo\n11,576\n57.5%\n10,099\n56.9%\n1,477\n61.8%\n<0.001\nYes\n8,565\n42.5%\n7,653\n43.1%\n912\n38.2%\nMissing\n1\n0.0%\n1\n0.0%\n0\n0.0%\nSmoking\nCurrent\n1,327\n6.6%\n1,098\n6.2%\n229\n9.6%\n<0.001\nFormer\n6,445\n32.0%\n5,649\n31.8%\n796\n33.3%\nNever\n12,370\n61.4%\n11,006\n62.0%\n1,364\n57.1%\nSD, standard deviation; JPY, Japanese yen; MMSE, Mini-Mental State ExaminationDepressive symptoms were classified based on the 15-item Geriatric Depression Scale: ≤5, without depressive symptoms; ≥6, with depressive symptoms. Missing values of disease history: Hypertension, n = 1; Diabetes, n = 9; Stroke, n = 3; Respiratory disease, n = 7. Statistical analysis was performed using the t-test or the chi-square test.\nRegarding smoking history, 6.6% were current smokers, 32.0% were former smokers, and 61.4% had never smoked. The prevalence of depressive symptoms (GDS score ≥6) was 11.9%. Participants with depressive symptoms were significantly older, had a higher prevalence of a history of diseases, were more likely to have lower income, were more likely to live alone, exercised less frequently, consumed less alcohol, and were more likely to be smokers (all p < 0.05). Among former smokers, the mean duration since smoking cessation was 21.7 years (SD, 16.7). Participants who quit smoking before age 65 years had a substantially longer duration of abstinence (mean, 29.3 years [SD, 13.7]) than those who quit smoking at age ≥65 years (mean, 7.8 years [SD, 5.4]).\nParticipant characteristics according to smoking status are presented in Additional File\n2\n. Ever smokers were more likely to be male, have no regular exercise habits, and report lower sleep satisfaction than never smokers.\nAssociation between duration since smoking cessation and depressive symptoms\nWe employed restricted cubic spline analysis to examine the nonlinear association between duration since smoking cessation and depressive symptoms. After adjusting for age and sex, a longer duration since smoking cessation was associated with a lower probability of depressive symptoms (Fig.\n1\n).\nFig. 1\nAssociation between the probability of depressive symptoms and duration of smoking cessation\nThe association between the probability of depressive symptoms and duration of smoking cessation was analyzed using restricted cubic spline analysis, after adjusting for age and sex. Depressive symptoms were defined as a 15-item Geriatric Depression Scale score ≥6. The blue line indicates the probability of depressive symptoms, and the gray area represents the 95% confidence interval.\nFor each GDS-15 item, we evaluated whether the proportion of depressive responses changed with increasing duration since smoking cessation using the Cochran–Armitage trend test. Items belonging to the Dysphoric Mood (Q1, Q3, Q4, Q5, Q7, Q11, and Q15), Withdrawal–Apathy–Vigor (Q9 and Q13), and Hopelessness (Q8, Q12, and Q14) domains showed a significant trend, indicating that the proportion of depressive symptoms decreased with increasing duration since smoking cessation (Additional File\n3\n). In contrast, items related to anxiety (Q6) and memory problems (Q10) were not significantly associated with duration since smoking cessation.\nUsing a multivariable-adjusted model, we examined the association between duration since smoking cessation and depressive symptoms. Among former smokers with a duration since smoking cessation of <7 years, the odds of depressive symptoms did not differ significantly from those of current smokers (AOR: <4 years, 0.832; 4–6 years, 0.963; all p > 0.05; Table\n2\n).\n"
